# openOODA Ecosystem Functional Requirements & Capability Contracts
File: openOODA/requirements.oot

This document establishes the authoritative functional specifications and capability
contracts delivered across the 11 components of the openOODA polyrepo ecosystem.
Mechanical invariants and negative laws are governed exclusively by rules.oot.
Target architectural posture and grammar are defined in northstar.oot.
Release milestones and task backlogs are tracked in roadmap.oot.

## 1. The 10 Requirements — Ecosystem Capability Contracts

### Requirement 1 — Sovereign Compiler Pipeline (`oodac`)
  Delivers ahead-of-time verification and native binary compilation:
  - Multi-stage pipeline: lexing, concrete AST, Merkle AST subtree hashing,
    typechecking, borrow checking, and contract verification.
  - Product emission: LLVM IR (clang + oodar runtime). Residual backends
    ELF64/WASM exit 2 until a later named path. C99 is the oodar host
    runtime, not an oodac emit backend.
  - Generates machine-readable diagnostics with automated one-turn repairs.

### Requirement 2a — Language CLI Workflow Driver (`cli`)
  Delivers developer ergonomics and language-specific execution workflows:
  - Unified command dispatch: `build`, `run`, `test`, `fmt`, `install`,
    `update`, `upgrade`, `init`, `qa`, `context`, `fix`.
  - Stationary workspace topology: executes in-place with zero path drift.
  - Deterministic test orchestration with sequential double-run verification.

### Requirement 2b — Operational Router (`ooda`)
  Delivers backwards-compat routing for the legacy `ooda` binary surface:
  - Argv router that forwards to `cli`, `opm`, `lsp`, `mcp`, plus the legacy
    `mcp-merge` passthrough to `ooda-mcp mcp-merge`.
  - Owns the spawn dispatcher (`build/spawn.oo`) and compiler locator
    (`build/find_compiler.oo`).
  - No in-process driver work; every verb forwards via `process_exec`.

### Requirement 3 — Runtime Substrate & Security Sandbox (`oodar`)
  Delivers low-level host runtime primitives in isolated C translation units:
  - Sandboxed execution: Landlock LSM filesystem confinement and seccomp-bpf filters.
  - Security model: 26 substrate capability bits (20 language tokens subset).
  - Linear arena allocator: contiguous memory layout with O(1) bulk resets.
  - Crash flight sensor: zero-heap signal interceptor and autopsy emission.

### Requirement 4 — Modular Standard Library (`std`)
  Delivers pure, idiomatic standard library modules across 8 functional domains:
  - Domains: `core`, `fs`, `net`, `sec`, `science`, `app`, `hw`, `meta`.
  - Zero-heap allocation: arena-backed data structures and zero-copy buffer slicing.
  - Modular encapsulation: strict <= 256 lines per file and 4-element Academy headers.

### Requirement 5 — Flight Recorder & Crash Autopsy Subsystem (`bb`)
  Delivers agent-native execution recording and post-mortem analysis:
  - In-memory ring buffer: zero-allocation telemetry recording during execution.
  - Signal interception: captures `SIGSEGV`, `SIGABRT`, `SIGBUS`, and `SIGILL`.
  - Diagnostic engine: parses `.blackbox/autopsy.json` for 1-turn root cause analysis.
  - Compiler stage diffing: AST semantic diff comparison via `bb diff`.

### Requirement 6 — Language Server Protocol Daemon (`lsp`)
  Delivers rich editor integration conforming to the JSON-RPC 2.0 protocol:
  - Language features: real-time diagnostics, hover tooltips, completion, definition jumps.
  - Capability inspection: static visibility into required capability tokens.

### Requirement 7 — Model Context Protocol Server (`mcp`)
  Delivers structured tool interfaces for autonomous AI agents:
  - Tool dispatch: exposes AST slicing, build verification, and rule checking.
  - Capability attenuation: dynamically constrains agent permissions per turn.

### Requirement 8 — Cryptographic Package Manager (`opm`)
  Delivers reproducible, verified dependency resolution and package distribution:
  - Manifest parsing: validates `ooda.pkg` manifests and lockfiles.
  - Cryptographic verification: minisign signature and SHA-256 integrity validation.
  - Capability auditing: verifies dependency capability declarations ahead-of-time.

### Requirement 9 — Central Package Index & Ecosystem Catalog (`catalog`)
  Delivers public registry metadata, package discovery, and security proofs:
  - Curated registry of verified openOODA libraries and tools.
  - Automated capability auditing and tamper-proof cryptographic proofs.

### Requirement 10 — Machine Landing & Web Platform (`install` & `website`)
  Delivers seamless installation, system integration, and documentation:
  - Multi-platform installer (`install.sh`, apt, dnf, pacman): idempotent landing,
    pre-flight dependency checks, fail-closed SHA-256 sidecars, and clean uninstalls.
  - Web platform (`website`): official documentation, architecture portals, and demos.

## 2. Subsystem Interface Contracts

### 2.1 Standard Exit Codes
  All CLI entrypoints (`oodac`, `ooda`, `bb`, `opm`) conform to standard exit codes:
  - `0`: Success (all operations, checks, or tests passed).
  - `1`: Execution failure (runtime exception, test failure, build error).
  - `2`: Invocation error (invalid CLI flags, unrecognized arguments, syntax error).
  - `3`: Capability violation (unauthorized operation, sandbox escape attempt, `E_CAP`).

### 2.2 IPC Protocols & Wire Schemas
  - Language Server Protocol: JSON-RPC 2.0 over standard I/O for editor communication.
  - Model Context Protocol: MCP standard JSON schemas for agent tool invocation.
  - Crash Autopsy Format: Structured JSON schema written to `.blackbox/autopsy.json`.

### 2.3 Environment Variable Contract
  - `OODA_COMPILER`: Absolute path to the sovereign compiler binary executable.
  - `OODA_REPO_ROOT`: Absolute path to the polyrepo root directory.
  - `OODA_FS_READDIR`: Colon-separated whitelist paths permitted for filesystem reads.
  - `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`: Colon-separated whitelist paths permitted for filesystem writes.

## 3. Boundary Demarcation & Out-of-Scope

  The functional requirements define what the ecosystem delivers. They do not cover:
  - Negative constitutional invariants and mechanical limits (governed by rules.oot).
  - Target language grammar, AST schemas, and type system vision (governed by northstar.oot).
  - Ephemeral release milestones and sprint backlogs (governed by roadmap.oot).
